   122	
   123	## 7. An all-degree coefficient-one boundary theorem
   124	
   125	**Theorem.** Let J be a set of indices and i an additional index. Suppose the principal GCM A_J is symmetrizable. For every positive degree alpha supported on J union {i} with alpha_i=1, the Serre ideal and maximal Cartan-disjoint ideal agree: I_alpha=K_alpha.
   126	
   127	This is a consequence of established symmetrizable theory, not a claim to solve the nonsymmetrizable conjecture. It is proved uniformly in all remaining coefficients of alpha and all allowed entries of A.
   128	
   129	**Proof by symmetrizable comparison.** We may restrict to the supported principal submatrix: free positive degrees, Serre spans, and lowerings with indices in the support are unchanged, while outside lowerings are zero.
   130	
   131	Choose positive integers d_j symmetrizing A_J. Such an integer choice exists after clearing denominators of a positive rational symmetrizer. On J union {i}, construct a GCM B by retaining every row indexed by J and putting
   132	
   133	    b_ij=d_j a_ji for j in J,   b_ii=2.
   134	
   135	Then diag((d_j)_(j in J),1) symmetrizes B. Its entries are integers with the required signs and symmetric zero pattern.
   136	
   137	Let S(A) and S(B) denote the Serre quotients, not the maximal-ideal quotients. Their positive spaces with coefficient of i equal to one are canonically identical. Indeed, all Serre generators whose leading index lies in J are unchanged. If a_ij<0, a Serre generator with leading index i contains at least two occurrences of e_i, so it and all positive descendants cannot contribute to an i-coefficient-one component. If a_ij=0, then a_ji=b_ij=0 and the commutation relation [e_i,e_j]=0 is unchanged. This proves equality of the defining positive ideal components in the entire slice, not just in a truncated degree.
   138	
   139	Under this identification, each lowering D_j with j in J is unchanged, because its formula depends only on row j of the matrix. Let W be the image in this slice of the extra ideal tau(A)/I(A). It is graded, stable under all D_j with j in J, and its component at epsilon_i is zero.
   140	
   141	By the Gabber–Kac theorem for the symmetrizable B, S(B)=g(B). Its invariant symmetric bilinear form gives a nondegenerate pairing between each nonzero root space g(B)_alpha and its opposite g(B)_(-alpha). For x in W_alpha, every element of the opposite root space is a linear combination of iterated brackets
