    57	## State of the art
    58	
    59	1. **Symmetrizable case:** Gabber–Kac is established. Every rank-two GCM is symmetrizable, and so is a GCM whose underlying graph is a forest, by consistent diagonal ratios along edges. Nonsymmetrizability first requires an unbalanced cycle; rank three is the smallest possible rank. These observations are not new nonsymmetrizable cases of the conjecture.
    60	2. **No all-height nonsymmetrizable case established by the opened literature:** the source audit found no proof for a nonsymmetrizable matrix over C and no characteristic-zero counterexample. This is a bounded search finding, not a theorem that no such paper exists. Hyperbolic classification, low rank, and restrictions on a_ij a_ji alone must not be promoted to a Gabber–Kac result.
    61	3. **Marquis's group counterexamples have the wrong characteristic for this problem:** [arXiv:1509.01976](https://arxiv.org/abs/1509.01976), published 2019, gives non-GK-simple group examples over finite fields. Its characteristic-zero Lie theorem remains conditional on symmetrizability. In §2.7 the example involving sl_m(k) and p dividing m is likewise not over C.
    62	4. **Kumar's geometry does not settle this conjecture:** Heckenberger–Kolb, [arXiv:math/0605460](https://arxiv.org/abs/math/0605460), [DOI 10.1007/s00031-007-0059-2](https://doi.org/10.1007/s00031-007-0059-2), Theorem 3.2, cites exactness of Kumar's BGG complex ending in the maximal integrable quotient V(mu). The same page states that simplicity of V(mu) is unknown for arbitrary Kac–Moody algebras. Exactness is not a proof that V(mu) is the simple module. The book calls the representation conjecture closely related; no unverified two-way equivalence is used here.
    63	5. **Quantum and super cases are not replacements:** [arXiv:1303.5994](https://arxiv.org/abs/1303.5994) is by **Xin Fang**, not Berenstein–Greenstein. Its defining Nichols ideals and failed specializations do not refute the classical Serre conjecture. Gorelik–Hinich–Serganova's gl(1|1), sl(n|n), sq(n) and other superalgebra exceptions have parity hypotheses absent from the book's ordinary Lie algebra problem.
