proof.md:48:**Proof.** First let K+=direct-sum_alpha K_alpha. By definition it is stable under each f_i; at height one it has no vectors, so lowering never produces a nonzero Cartan component. It is stable under h because each homogeneous component is a weight space.
proof.md:66:**Proof.** The right-hand recursive family contains the prescribed generators and is stable under bracket with every e_i. Repeated Jacobi identities imply stability under bracket with every element of the Lie algebra generated by the e_i, namely L. It is therefore an ideal containing all s_ij, and contains I by minimality. The reverse containment follows by induction, since every displayed generator/bracket belongs to I. QED.
proof.md:101:The injectivity test proves K_alpha=I_alpha by induction: Section 4 already proves lowering-stability of I, and lower-degree equality identifies the map's targets with the actual maximal quotients. Therefore its kernel is precisely K_alpha/I_alpha. Checking only quotient columns omits no possible extra relation; it avoids rechecking a large ideal whose inclusion was proved algebraically. The simple-root case remains explicitly separate.
proof.md:121:The initial finite runs target **all** positive multidegrees of height at most 10 for all five matrices, not just diagonal degrees. A separately recorded extension targets all positive degrees through height 12 for A_one. A missing free-Lie degree has zero dimension and is treated as zero throughout. The exact run and independent-check receipts determine the accepted bound; this file does not claim any larger unexercised range.
proof.md:135:Then diag((d_j)_(j in J),1) symmetrizes B. Its entries are integers with the required signs and symmetric zero pattern.
proof.md:137:Let S(A) and S(B) denote the Serre quotients, not the maximal-ideal quotients. Their positive spaces with coefficient of i equal to one are canonically identical. Indeed, all Serre generators whose leading index lies in J are unchanged. If a_ij<0, a Serre generator with leading index i contains at least two occurrences of e_i, so it and all positive descendants cannot contribute to an i-coefficient-one component. If a_ij=0, then a_ji=b_ij=0 and the commutation relation [e_i,e_j]=0 is unchanged. This proves equality of the defining positive ideal components in the entire slice, not just in a truncated degree.
