evidence/descent-audit.md:191:where `g(A)` now has the book's maximal-ideal definition. The positive component of the Serre quotient is `L_alpha/I_alpha`. The natural surjection from the latter to the former is an isomorphism exactly when `I_alpha=K_alpha`.
evidence/descent-audit.md:195:It is **not** legitimate to call the spaces of the full algebra of absolute height at most `N` a quotient Lie algebra obtained by throwing away all larger degrees: lowering sends degree `N+1` to degree `N`, so the discarded full-algebra subspace is not generally an ideal. The positive algebra alone does have its usual quotient by positive degrees greater than `N`, but the full lowering action need not descend to that positive truncation.
evidence/descent-audit.md:378:Use integral Lyndon/Hall bases of the free Lie algebra. All bracket and lowering matrices then have integer entries. Reduction modulo a prime can lower matrix rank, enlarge a lowering kernel, or lower the rank of the generated Serre subspace. Integral ideal lattices need not be saturated, and rational quotient bases cannot be reduced at primes dividing their denominators without additional care.
